# Real Lightroom evidence, for the `#[ignore]`d tests only.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the pairs your machine holds; one gate per
# pair. `.env` is gitignored: real catalog paths and real catalog bytes never
# enter the repo.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   sh scripts/check.sh real
#
# Every worktree on a machine needs this gate, so a second route exists that no
# worktree owns: put the same two names in `~/.config/lrsalvage/real.env` (or the
# file named by `git config lrsalvage.realEnvFile`). A worktree's own `.env` wins
# where it exists; the machine's file is the fallback.
#
# The catalog and its cache must be the same pair — `<name>.lrcat` beside
# `<name> Previews.lrdata` — and only that pair is opened, read-only.
#
# What these tests are for: they are the only source of the "verified" rows in
# README.md's compatibility table, and of the evidence labels in
# docs/format-notes.md. A layout measured from real files is a fact; the same
# layout asserted from a synthetic vault is a decision. Do not upgrade a claim
# without running these.

LRSALVAGE_TEST_CATALOG=""
LRSALVAGE_TEST_PREVIEWS=""

# A cache Lightroom **rendered** from scratch, for the `real-render` gate:
#
#   sh scripts/check.sh real-render
#
# `check.sh real` above covers a converted cache. A render holds two pyramids per
# image — the preview pyramid and an unclaimed thumbnail pyramid beside it — which
# is the state `format-notes` §5.10 measured and ADR 0008 decides a rule for. The
# gate asserts those published numbers and that no companion is ever written.
# Leave these empty where no rendered cache exists: `real-render` then fails and
# says so, rather than passing on evidence it did not check.

LRSALVAGE_RENDER_CATALOG=""
LRSALVAGE_RENDER_PREVIEWS=""

# A cache Lightroom **built 1:1 previews** for, for the `real-1to1` gate:
#
#   sh scripts/check.sh real-1to1
#
# A 1:1 build *replaces* the standard pyramid rather than adding to it, so such a
# cache holds one pyramid per image, no companion, and no bare level file — the
# third of `format-notes` §5.10's three caches, and the only one where the
# companion ADR 0008 and ADR 0009 decide about is absent entirely. The gate
# asserts those published numbers and that an extract still writes one file per
# image. Leave these empty where no such cache exists: `real-1to1` then fails and
# says so, rather than passing on evidence it did not check.

LRSALVAGE_ONE_TO_ONE_CATALOG=""
LRSALVAGE_ONE_TO_ONE_PREVIEWS=""

# A cache Lightroom **discarded the standard and 1:1 previews of**, for the
# `real-discard` gate:
#
#   sh scripts/check.sh real-discard
#
# `Library > Previews > Discard Standard and 1:1 Previews` is the one deletion a
# user can trigger, and what it leaves is the state `format-notes` §5.11 measured:
# every preview pyramid shrunk rather than emptied, no companion left, and
# `quality` rewritten to describe the levels a pyramid still holds. The gate
# asserts those published counts and that an extract still writes one file per
# image.
#
# The copy these name is the one Adobe wrote the deletion into, not the render it
# was cut from, and it must live outside any scratch tree with an `EVIDENCE.md`
# beside it — the recipe, and what that file has to record, are in
# `docs/agents/rendering-a-test-cache.md`. Leave these empty where no such copy
# exists: `real-discard` then fails and says so, rather than passing on evidence
# it did not check.

LRSALVAGE_DISCARD_CATALOG=""
LRSALVAGE_DISCARD_PREVIEWS=""

# The wasm32 C toolchain, for the `wasm` gate only:
#
#   sh scripts/check.sh wasm
#
# `lrsalvage-core` is also compiled for `wasm32-unknown-unknown`, and
# `sqlite-wasm-rs` builds SQLite's amalgamation with `cc`, so that target needs a
# C compiler for wasm. `cc` finds `clang` on PATH where a machine has one — CI's
# runner does, and needs nothing here — so these two names are only for a
# toolchain installed somewhere else:
#
#   CC_wasm32_unknown_unknown="/path/to/wasi-sdk/bin/clang"
#   AR_wasm32_unknown_unknown="/path/to/wasi-sdk/bin/llvm-ar"
#
# They stay commented out because an empty value is not an unset one: `cc` would
# try to run the empty string, and a machine that has clang on PATH would fail
# instead of using it. No test reads them; they are here because this is one
# gitignored file a machine's own paths may live in, and a gate that cannot find
# its compiler fails rather than skipping.
#
# They belong in `~/.config/lrsalvage/real.env`, the machine file — not in a
# worktree's `.env`. A worktree `.env` wins over that file wholesale, so one
# carrying only these two names hides every catalog pair above and turns the four
# evidence gates red on a machine that holds all four, saying "set
# LRSALVAGE_…" rather than naming the file it never read. The machine file is
# also what every worktree reads, so the wasm gate then needs no per-checkout
# setup.
